(function () {
  const L12 = [
    { band: "1", marks: "1-3", score: 2, desc: "A few relevant points, mostly listed or asserted, with little or no explanation. No clear structure or line of reasoning." },
    { band: "2", marks: "4-6", score: 5, desc: "Some relevant points with some explanation, but narrow (only one or two of the required areas) or largely one-sided. Limited attempt at a line of reasoning; few specific examples." },
    { band: "3", marks: "7-9", score: 8, desc: "A line of reasoning is followed. Developed points covering most of the required areas (or all, with some brief). Some specific examples and some balance; a conclusion may be present but not fully justified." },
    { band: "4", marks: "10-12", score: 11, desc: "A coherent, relevant, substantiated and logically structured response. **All** required areas covered in depth and applied to the scenario, balanced with counter-arguments, specific examples/legislation, and a justified conclusion." },
  ];
  const L9 = [
    { band: "1", marks: "1-3", score: 2, desc: "A few relevant points, mostly stated, with little explanation or structure." },
    { band: "2", marks: "4-6", score: 5, desc: "Some explained points, but limited in range or depth or one-sided; limited reasoning." },
    { band: "3", marks: "7-9", score: 8, desc: "A logically structured, balanced response with developed points and specific examples covering the required areas, leading to a justified conclusion." },
  ];
  CS.registerExam("8.3", [
    { marks: 12, q: "Many homes now have **smart speakers** that respond to voice commands. Discuss the **moral, ethical, legal and cultural** issues raised by their use.", levels: L12, points: [
      "Benefits: convenience; accessibility for blind, elderly or physically disabled users; home automation; information and companionship",
      "Ethical/privacy: always-listening microphones; accidental activations record private conversations; recordings reviewed by human contractors; profiling for advertising",
      "Ethical: children's voices and data collected; children may not understand; Children's Code / age-appropriate design",
      "Legal: UK GDPR — transparency, purpose limitation, data minimisation, retention limits, security; voice may be biometric data; right of access/erasure",
      "Legal: police requests for recordings as evidence (IPA 2016 / warrants); hacking of devices (Computer Misuse Act); PSTI Act 2022 bans universal default passwords",
      "Moral: individuals deciding whether to record guests/visitors without their knowledge; developers' responsibility for privacy-by-default design",
      "Cultural: changing family interaction and how children learn and ask questions; normalising surveillance in the home; dominance of a few platforms and languages/accents recognised poorly",
      "Justified conclusion, e.g. benefits outweigh risks if users control retention, mute is easy, data minimised and not used for unrelated advertising"
    ], guidance: "Level 4 requires all four areas, applied to smart speakers, with a justified conclusion.",
    exemplars: [
      { level: 1, mark: 3, answer: "Smart speakers are useful because you can ask them questions, play music and control your lights with your voice. This is especially good for disabled people.\n\nHowever, smart speakers are always listening, so they could record everything you say. This is an invasion of privacy. Companies could sell your data to advertisers. Hackers could also hack into them.\n\nOverall I think smart speakers are good but people should be careful.", comment: "Relevant points on both sides are stated, including accessibility and privacy, but each is asserted in a sentence without explanation, and there is no reference to moral, legal or cultural issues as such, which is Level 1. \"Always listening, so they could record everything\" is misleading: the device listens locally for the wake word and normally only sends audio after activation, although accidental activations do send unintended recordings. Explaining points across at least two of the required areas would reach Level 2." },
      { level: 2, mark: 6, answer: "Smart speakers such as Amazon Echo and Google Nest are very popular and they have many benefits. They are convenient because people can set timers, check the weather, play music or control smart lights and heating using only their voice. They can also be used to order shopping or call family members. They are especially helpful for people who are blind or partially sighted, elderly people and people with physical disabilities who might find it hard to use a phone or computer. Some elderly people who live alone also use them for company or to call for help.\n\nHowever, there are serious privacy issues. Smart speakers have microphones that are always switched on, listening for the wake word such as \"Alexa\". Sometimes they think they have heard the wake word when they haven't, and they start recording a private conversation and send it to the company's servers. In 2019 it was reported that companies including Amazon and Apple employed contractors who listened to some of these recordings to improve the voice recognition, and some of the recordings included very personal information such as medical details and arguments. Many users did not know that humans might listen to their recordings.\n\nThe companies can also use the data collected from smart speakers to build up a profile of the user, such as what music they like, what they buy and what time they get up, and use this to target adverts at them. This is an ethical issue because people may not have agreed to this or understood it when they set up the device. Children also use smart speakers and they may not understand that their voices are being recorded and stored.\n\nSmart speakers could also be hacked, and a hacker could listen in to the house. Another problem is that smart speakers are often connected to other smart devices in the home such as door locks, cameras and heating. If someone hacked the speaker or the account it is linked to, they might be able to control these devices, for example unlocking the front door, which would be a serious security risk as well as a privacy one.\n\nIn conclusion, smart speakers are useful, but there are big privacy issues and people should check their privacy settings.", comment: "Benefits and ethical/privacy issues are explained with some development and a good specific example (contractors reviewing recordings in 2019), which puts this at the top of Level 2. It remains narrow: legal issues are reduced to an unnamed mention of hacking, there is nothing moral or cultural, and the conclusion is not reasoned. Covering UK GDPR, the Children's Code or the PSTI Act, plus moral and cultural effects, would reach Level 3." },
      { level: 3, mark: 8, answer: "Smart speakers bring real benefits: hands-free control of lights and heating, information on demand, and genuine independence for blind, elderly or physically disabled users.\n\n**Ethical.** The device listens constantly for its wake word, and accidental activations mean private conversations can be recorded and uploaded. In 2019 it emerged that contractors for several companies, including Amazon and Apple, were reviewing samples of recordings, which most users had not realised. Recordings and usage patterns can also be used to profile households for advertising. Children are a particular concern because they use the devices but cannot meaningfully consent.\n\n**Legal.** Recordings of identifiable people are personal data under UK GDPR, so companies must be transparent, collect only what they need, keep it no longer than necessary and secure it. Users have a right of access and can ask for recordings to be erased. The ICO's Children's Code requires services likely to be used by children to have high-privacy default settings. Police may also seek recordings as evidence with appropriate legal authority.\n\n**Moral.** Owners have to decide whether it is right to have a device recording in rooms where guests, who have not agreed to it, are talking.\n\n**Cultural.** Speakers change how families interact and how children learn to ask questions, and they normalise having a microphone in the home.\n\nOn balance, smart speakers are worthwhile, provided users understand and control what is recorded.", comment: "A line of reasoning is followed, with developed ethical and legal points and some specific examples, and moral and cultural issues are present but brief, which is the Level 3 descriptor. The conclusion is reasonable but not justified by weighing the issues raised. Developing the moral and cultural areas in depth (e.g. dominance of a few platforms, accents recognised poorly) and justifying the conclusion would reach Level 4." },
      { level: 4, mark: 11, answer: "Smart speakers offer convenience and, more importantly, accessibility: a blind or elderly user can make calls, set reminders and control heating by voice, which can mean real independence. Against this are significant concerns.\n\n**Ethical.** Speakers listen locally for a wake word, but false activations send snippets of private conversation to the company's servers. In 2019 it emerged that contractors for Amazon, Apple and others reviewed samples of recordings, some including medical details, without most users realising. Voice data and usage patterns can be combined to profile a household for targeted advertising. Children talk to speakers freely but cannot understand or consent to how their data is used.\n\n**Legal.** Recordings are personal data under UK GDPR, so manufacturers must be transparent, minimise what they collect, limit retention and keep it secure; users have rights of access and erasure. A voice processed to identify a specific person can count as biometric, special category data. The ICO's Children's Code requires high-privacy defaults for services children are likely to use. Since 2024 the Product Security and Telecommunications Infrastructure Act bans universal default passwords on smart devices, reducing the risk of hacking, which would itself be an offence under the Computer Misuse Act. Police can seek recordings as evidence, as happened in a US murder investigation, raising the question of whether a home device can become a witness.\n\n**Moral.** Owners must decide whether it is right to record guests, cleaners or babysitters who haven't agreed. Developers have a moral responsibility to choose privacy-protective defaults, such as short retention and easy mute switches, even if that means less data for their products.\n\n**Cultural.** Speakers change family life: children may expect instant answers and speak to devices in commanding ways. Having a microphone in the living room becomes normal. A handful of US companies control the platforms, and some accents and languages are recognised less accurately, so the benefits are not shared equally.\n\nOn balance, the accessibility benefits are strong enough that smart speakers are worth having, but only if users control retention and can easily delete recordings, data is minimised and not used for unrelated advertising, and children's data is given extra protection. Those conditions should be the default, not settings users have to find.", comment: "All four areas are covered in depth and applied to smart speakers, with counter-arguments, accurate legislation (UK GDPR, Children's Code, PSTI Act, Computer Misuse Act) and a conclusion justified by the issues raised: Level 4. It is 11 rather than 12 because the US police example is vague; naming it precisely, or using a UK example, would fully substantiate that point." }
    ] },
    { marks: 12, q: "Discuss the **moral, ethical, legal and cultural** issues associated with the introduction of **fully autonomous (self-driving) vehicles** on public roads.", levels: L12, points: [
      "Benefits: most collisions involve human error → potential to save lives; independence for elderly/disabled people; smoother traffic, lower emissions; productivity during journeys",
      "Ethical: programmed trade-offs in unavoidable collisions embed values (who is protected?); cultural variation (Moral Machine study)",
      "Ethical/accountability: who is responsible when a crash happens — manufacturer, software developer, owner? Uber 2018 fatality in Arizona (safety driver prosecuted)",
      "Legal: Automated Vehicles Act 2024 — authorised self-driving entity responsible; user-in-charge immune from offences arising from the way the vehicle drives while self-driving (still responsible for e.g. insurance, roadworthiness)",
      "Legal: hacking a vehicle is a CMA offence (s3/s3ZA); location and journey data is personal data under UK GDPR",
      "Moral: engineers deciding whether a system is safe enough to release; pressure of commercial deadlines; BCS duty to public interest",
      "Economic/ethical: job losses for taxi, lorry and delivery drivers; need for retraining",
      "Cultural: changing relationship with cars and driving, town planning, trust in machines; digital divide in access",
      "Justified conclusion"
    ],
    exemplars: [
      { level: 1, mark: 2, answer: "Self-driving cars could be dangerous if the software goes wrong and crashes. Also if a car crashes it isn't clear who is to blame. Taxi drivers would lose their jobs.\n\nBut they could also be safer because computers don't get tired.", comment: "Relevant points on both sides are asserted in single sentences without explanation, examples or structure, which is Level 1. None of the required areas is named or developed. Explaining how responsibility for a crash could be decided, with an example or the relevant law, and developing at least one other area would reach Level 2." },
      { level: 2, mark: 5, answer: "Self-driving cars have many advantages. Most car accidents are caused by human error such as speeding, drink driving, being distracted by a phone or falling asleep. A self-driving car would not do any of these things, so there could be far fewer accidents and many lives could be saved. Self-driving cars would also give independence to people who cannot drive, such as elderly or disabled people. They could reduce traffic jams because the cars could communicate with each other and drive more smoothly, and passengers could work or relax during the journey.\n\nThere are also ethical issues. If a self-driving car is about to crash and cannot avoid it, the software has to decide what to do, for example whether to swerve and hit a pedestrian or crash and injure the passenger. Programmers have to decide this in advance, which means they are deciding whose life is more important. This is similar to the trolley problem. Different people would make different decisions.\n\nAnother ethical issue is who is responsible if a self-driving car crashes. It could be the owner, the manufacturer or the programmer who wrote the software. In 2018 a self-driving Uber car hit and killed a woman in Arizona.\n\nSelf-driving cars would also mean that lorry drivers and taxi drivers lose their jobs.\n\nOverall I think the benefits of self-driving cars outweigh the problems.", comment: "Benefits and ethical issues are explained with some development and one specific example (the 2018 Uber fatality), which is Level 2. The answer is narrow: there is no legal discussion (e.g. the Automated Vehicles Act 2024 on who is responsible), nothing moral or cultural, and the conclusion is asserted rather than weighed. Covering most of the four areas with developed points would reach Level 3." },
      { level: 3, mark: 8, answer: "Autonomous vehicles could save many lives, since most collisions involve human error such as distraction or tiredness, and they would give independence to people who cannot drive.\n\n**Ethical.** In an unavoidable crash the software must choose an action, so developers effectively decide in advance whose safety is prioritised. MIT's Moral Machine study found people in different cultures answer these dilemmas differently, so there is no agreed \"right\" setting. Accountability is also unclear: when an Uber test vehicle killed a pedestrian in Arizona in 2018, it was the safety driver who was prosecuted.\n\n**Legal.** The Automated Vehicles Act 2024 addresses this in the UK: when a vehicle is driving itself, the authorised self-driving entity (usually the manufacturer) is responsible for how it drives, and the user-in-charge is not liable for offences arising from the way it drives. Hacking a vehicle would be an offence under the Computer Misuse Act, and journey and location data is personal data under UK GDPR.\n\n**Moral.** Engineers must decide when the system is safe enough to release, under pressure from deadlines.\n\n**Cultural.** Driving is part of many people's identity, and people may not trust machines at first.\n\nTaxi, lorry and delivery drivers could also lose their jobs.\n\nOverall, self-driving cars should be introduced once they are proven to be safer than human drivers.", comment: "A clear line of reasoning with developed ethical and legal points supported by accurate examples (Moral Machine, Uber 2018, Automated Vehicles Act 2024), while moral and cultural points are brief, which is Level 3. The conclusion introduces a sensible test but does not weigh the issues discussed. Developing the moral and cultural areas (e.g. professional duty under the BCS Code, effects on town planning and the digital divide) and justifying the conclusion would reach Level 4." },
      { level: 4, mark: 12, answer: "Most road collisions involve human error such as distraction, speeding or fatigue, so autonomous vehicles could save many lives. They could also give independence to elderly and disabled people, smooth traffic flow and cut emissions. These benefits make the issues worth taking seriously rather than dismissing the technology.\n\n**Ethical.** In an unavoidable collision the software must act, so developers embed value judgements in advance, for example whether to protect occupants or pedestrians. MIT's Moral Machine experiment showed preferences vary between cultures, so whatever is chosen will be contested. Accountability is equally hard: after an Uber test vehicle killed a pedestrian in Arizona in 2018, it was the human safety driver who was prosecuted, even though the system had failed to identify her correctly.\n\n**Legal.** In the UK the Automated Vehicles Act 2024 clarifies liability: while a vehicle is driving itself, an authorised self-driving entity, usually the manufacturer, is responsible for how it drives, and the user-in-charge is immune from prosecution for offences arising from the way the vehicle drives. That gives manufacturers a strong incentive to make systems safe. Hacking a vehicle would be an offence under the Computer Misuse Act, and the location and journey data cars collect is personal data under UK GDPR, so its use must be limited and transparent.\n\n**Moral.** Individual engineers face the question of when a system is \"safe enough\" to release while under commercial pressure. The BCS Code of Conduct requires them to put the public interest, including safety, first, which could mean refusing to sign off a system they have doubts about.\n\n**Economic and ethical.** Hundreds of thousands of taxi, lorry and delivery drivers could lose their jobs. Society has a responsibility to provide retraining rather than leave the benefits to manufacturers and the costs to workers.\n\n**Cultural.** For many people driving represents freedom and skill, so trust in machines may be slow to build. Towns could change, with less parking and more shared fleets, but if autonomous travel is expensive, people in poorer or rural areas could be left behind, widening the digital divide.\n\nOverall, autonomous vehicles should be introduced, because the potential to reduce deaths caused by human error is too large to ignore. But this should happen gradually, with independent safety approval, clear manufacturer liability as the 2024 Act provides, and support for workers who lose their jobs.", comment: "All four areas are covered in depth and applied to autonomous vehicles, balanced against clear benefits, with accurate legislation and examples (Automated Vehicles Act 2024, Uber 2018, Moral Machine, BCS Code), leading to a conclusion that follows from the discussion. This meets every part of the Level 4 descriptor and earns full marks." }
    ] },
    { marks: 9, q: "Some governments want technology companies to give law enforcement access to **end-to-end encrypted** messages. Discuss the arguments for and against this.", levels: L9, points: [
      "For: helps prevent and investigate terrorism, child sexual abuse and organised crime; criminals 'going dark'; evidence for prosecutions",
      "For: existing legal frameworks (IPA 2016 warrants, double lock; RIPA Part III key disclosure) could provide oversight",
      "Against: a back door for police is a vulnerability others (criminals, hostile states) can exploit — weakens security for everyone",
      "Against: protects journalists, whistleblowers, abuse victims, dissidents under repressive regimes; chilling effect",
      "Against: criminals can switch to other tools/apps, so the measure mainly affects law-abiding users",
      "Examples: Apple v FBI (San Bernardino, 2016); Apple withdrawing Advanced Data Protection in the UK (2025); WhatsApp/Signal threats to leave the UK over the Online Safety Act; police infiltration of EncroChat (2020)",
      "Jurisdiction: global products; one country's demand affects users worldwide",
      "Justified conclusion, e.g. targeted device access with judicial warrants preferable to weakening encryption for all"
    ],
    exemplars: [
      { level: 1, mark: 2, answer: "Governments should be able to read encrypted messages because criminals and terrorists use apps like WhatsApp to plan crimes. If the police can read the messages they can stop them.\n\nHowever, people have a right to privacy.", comment: "One argument for and one against are stated, but neither is explained and there is no structure or example, which is Level 1. Explaining why access is wanted, and why a back door might weaken security for everyone, would reach Level 2." },
      { level: 2, mark: 5, answer: "End-to-end encryption means that a message is encrypted on the sender's device and can only be decrypted on the recipient's device. Not even the company that runs the service, such as WhatsApp or Signal, can read the messages, because it does not have the keys. Only the two people in the conversation can read them.\n\nThere are strong arguments for giving law enforcement access. Terrorists, people who share child abuse images and organised criminal gangs all use end-to-end encrypted apps to communicate because they know that the police cannot read their messages. This is called \"going dark\". If the police could read these messages, they would be able to stop terrorist attacks before they happen, rescue children who are being abused and catch drug dealers. The messages could also be used as evidence in court to help convict criminals. Governments argue that the police can already get a warrant to search someone's house or listen to their phone calls, so it doesn't make sense that they can't read their messages.\n\nThere are also arguments against. Many people believe that they have a right to privacy and that the government should not be able to read their private messages. Some people don't trust the government with this power.\n\nIn my opinion, governments should be given access to encrypted messages because keeping people safe from terrorism is more important than privacy.", comment: "End-to-end encryption and the case for access are explained well, but the answer is largely one-sided: the arguments against are reduced to privacy and trust, missing the central technical point that a back door for police is a vulnerability others can exploit. There are no specific examples and the conclusion simply restates one side, so it stays in Level 2. Developing the security and civil-liberty counter-arguments with examples (e.g. Apple v FBI) and weighing them would reach Level 3." },
      { level: 3, mark: 8, answer: "With end-to-end encryption (E2EE), only the sender and recipient hold the keys, so not even the service provider can read messages.\n\n**For access.** Terrorists, organised crime groups and people sharing child sexual abuse material use E2EE apps, so police risk \"going dark\" on evidence they could previously obtain with a warrant. Supporters argue the UK already has legal safeguards: interception under the Investigatory Powers Act 2016 needs a warrant approved by both a minister and a judicial commissioner (the \"double lock\"), so access could be overseen in the same way.\n\n**Against.** There is no way to build a back door that only the \"good guys\" can use. Any mechanism that lets police decrypt messages is a vulnerability that criminals or hostile states could find and exploit, weakening security for every user, including banks and businesses. E2EE also protects journalists, whistleblowers, domestic abuse victims and dissidents in repressive countries. Serious criminals could simply move to other encrypted tools, so the main effect would be on law-abiding people. Apple refused to help the FBI unlock a terrorist's iPhone in 2016 for this reason, and in 2025 withdrew its Advanced Data Protection feature from the UK rather than weaken it. Products are global, so one country's demands can affect users everywhere.\n\nPolice have also succeeded without back doors: the infiltration of the EncroChat network in 2020 led to many arrests.\n\nI conclude that targeted measures, such as hacking a specific suspect's device under a judicial warrant, are better than weakening encryption for everyone, because they help investigations without creating a risk for every user.", comment: "A logically structured, balanced response with developed arguments on both sides, accurate specific examples (IPA 2016 double lock, Apple v FBI, Apple ADP 2025, EncroChat) and a justified conclusion: Level 3. It is 8 rather than 9 because the \"for\" side is less fully developed than the \"against\" side, so the weighing behind the conclusion is slightly uneven." }
    ] },
    { marks: 12, q: "Discuss the impact of **social media platforms** on individuals and society, including the **responsibilities** of the computer scientists who design them.", levels: L12, points: [
      "Positive: connection and community; voice for marginalised groups; rapid information in emergencies; small businesses and creators",
      "Monitoring and profiling for targeted advertising; data harvesting (Cambridge Analytica 2018, up to 87m users; £500,000 ICO fine)",
      "Algorithms optimised for engagement embed values; amplify outrage/extreme content; filter bubbles and echo chambers",
      "Misinformation/disinformation spread at scale (e.g. 2024 Southport riots); deepfakes",
      "Health: persuasive design (infinite scroll, streaks); links to anxiety, sleep, body image in young people; Molly Russell inquest (2022)",
      "Legal: Online Safety Act 2023 — illegal content and children's safety duties, Ofcom fines up to £18m/10%; UK GDPR / Children's Code; TikTok £12.7m fine (2023)",
      "Free speech v protection from harm; who decides what is removed; moderation at scale",
      "Responsibilities of designers: BCS Code (public interest), safety by design, privacy by default, transparency, not exploiting psychological weaknesses",
      "Justified conclusion"
    ],
    exemplars: [
      { level: 1, mark: 2, answer: "Social media lets people keep in touch with friends and family all over the world. However, it can be bad for mental health and there is a lot of cyberbullying. There is also fake news.\n\nComputer scientists should make social media safer.", comment: "A few relevant points are listed on both sides without explanation, examples or structure, which is Level 1. The responsibilities of designers are reduced to a single vague sentence. Explaining how, for example, engagement-based algorithms spread misinformation, with a specific example, would reach Level 2." },
      { level: 2, mark: 6, answer: "Social media has had a huge impact on individuals and society, both positive and negative.\n\nOn the positive side, social media allows people to stay connected with friends and family wherever they are in the world. It lets people find communities of others with the same interests or experiences, which can be really important for people who feel isolated, for example LGBTQ+ young people or people with rare illnesses. Small businesses and creators can reach customers cheaply, and in emergencies information can be shared very quickly.\n\nOn the negative side, social media platforms collect a huge amount of data about their users, such as what they like, who their friends are and where they go. They use this data to show targeted adverts, which is how they make their money. In the Cambridge Analytica scandal in 2018, data from up to 87 million Facebook users was harvested without most of them knowing and used for political advertising.\n\nSocial media can also have a negative effect on mental health, especially for young people. Features like infinite scrolling, likes and streaks are designed to keep people on the app for as long as possible. Young people compare themselves to the perfect images they see, which can lead to anxiety and problems with body image. The inquest into the death of Molly Russell in 2022 found that harmful content she saw online contributed to her death.\n\nFake news also spreads very quickly on social media because algorithms show people content that gets lots of engagement, and shocking or angry posts get the most engagement.\n\nOverall social media has both good and bad effects.", comment: "Positive and negative impacts are explained with good specific examples (Cambridge Analytica, the Molly Russell inquest), which places it at the top of Level 2. The question also asks for the responsibilities of computer scientists, which are never discussed, there is no legal angle (e.g. the Online Safety Act 2023), and the conclusion does not weigh anything. Covering designers' responsibilities and the law, and reaching a justified conclusion, would move it to Level 3 or above." },
      { level: 3, mark: 9, answer: "Social media connects people across distances, gives a voice to marginalised groups and lets small businesses and creators reach audiences cheaply. In emergencies it spreads information very quickly.\n\nHowever, platforms make money by monitoring and profiling users for targeted advertising. In the Cambridge Analytica scandal, data from up to 87 million Facebook users was harvested and used for political targeting; the ICO fined Facebook £500,000.\n\nRecommendation algorithms are optimised for engagement, and outrage and extreme content engage people most, so it is amplified and users can end up in filter bubbles. Misinformation spreads at scale: during the 2024 Southport riots, false claims about the attacker's identity spread rapidly online.\n\nFor young people, persuasive design such as infinite scroll and streaks is linked to anxiety, poor sleep and body-image problems. The coroner at the Molly Russell inquest (2022) concluded that online content contributed to her death.\n\nThe law is catching up. The Online Safety Act 2023 gives platforms duties to tackle illegal content and protect children, with Ofcom able to fine up to £18 million or 10% of global turnover. TikTok was fined £12.7 million by the ICO in 2023 for misusing children's data.\n\nComputer scientists who design these platforms have a responsibility under the BCS Code to act in the public interest: designing for safety and privacy by default rather than maximum engagement.\n\nOverall social media has benefits, but its current design causes serious harm.", comment: "A clear line of reasoning with developed points and many accurate examples (Cambridge Analytica, Southport, Online Safety Act, TikTok fine) puts this at the top of Level 3. Free speech versus moderation is not considered, so the balance is limited, the designers' responsibilities get only one paragraph, and the conclusion is asserted rather than justified. Weighing free expression against harm and developing what responsible design would change would reach Level 4." },
      { level: 4, mark: 11, answer: "Social media has real benefits. It keeps families and friends connected across distances, gives marginalised groups a voice and a community, helps small businesses and creators reach customers, and spreads urgent information in emergencies.\n\nThe harms largely come from the business model. Platforms earn money from advertising, so they monitor and profile users. The Cambridge Analytica scandal (2018), in which data from up to 87 million Facebook users was harvested for political targeting, showed how that data can be misused; the ICO fined Facebook £500,000, the maximum under the old law.\n\nRecommendation algorithms are optimised for engagement, and outrage holds attention, so extreme content is amplified and users are pushed into filter bubbles. This is a design choice that embeds values. Misinformation spreads at a scale and speed traditional media could not match: false claims about the attacker's identity after the Southport stabbings in 2024 spread online within hours and contributed to riots.\n\nFor individuals, especially young people, persuasive design (infinite scroll, streaks, notifications) is linked to anxiety, poor sleep and body-image problems. The coroner at Molly Russell's inquest (2022) concluded that online content she had been shown contributed to her death.\n\nThe law now imposes duties. The Online Safety Act 2023 requires platforms to tackle illegal content and protect children, with Ofcom able to fine up to £18 million or 10% of global turnover, whichever is greater. UK GDPR and the ICO's Children's Code limit data use, and TikTok was fined £12.7 million in 2023 for processing children's data unlawfully.\n\nRegulation raises a genuine dilemma, though. Removing harmful content protects users, but over-zealous moderation, often automated, can silence legitimate speech, and someone has to decide where the line falls.\n\nComputer scientists carry real responsibility here. The BCS Code requires them to act in the public interest. In practice that means safety by design, privacy by default, being transparent about how feeds are ranked, and not exploiting psychological weaknesses to increase time on the app.\n\nOverall, social media's benefits are real, but its harms flow mainly from engagement-maximising design rather than from the technology itself. Designers and regulators therefore can reduce the harm without losing the benefits, and the Online Safety Act's focus on systems and design, rather than individual posts, is the right approach.", comment: "A coherent, logically structured response that covers individuals, society, law and designers' responsibilities in depth, with balance (free speech versus protection) and accurate, specific evidence, reaching a conclusion that follows from the argument: Level 4. It is 11 rather than 12 because the positive impacts are listed in one paragraph and not developed or weighed as carefully as the harms." }
    ] },
    { marks: 9, q: "The NHS wants to share **anonymised** patient records with technology companies to develop AI diagnostic tools. Discuss the issues this raises.", levels: L9, points: [
      "Benefits: earlier and more accurate diagnosis; research into treatments; reduced workload for clinicians; NHS data is a uniquely large, valuable dataset",
      "Re-identification risk: 'anonymised' data can be matched with other data to identify individuals",
      "Legal: health data is special category data; UK GDPR lawful basis, transparency, security, data minimisation; DPIA",
      "Trust and consent: patients may object to commercial use; opt-outs; transparency about which companies and why",
      "Ethical: private companies profiting from public data; fair return to the NHS",
      "Bias: if data is unrepresentative, tools may perform worse for some groups",
      "Accountability: who is liable if an AI-assisted diagnosis is wrong — company, clinician or NHS?",
      "Security of shared data; data breaches",
      "Justified conclusion, e.g. acceptable in a secure environment where data does not leave NHS control, with independent oversight and public transparency"
    ],
    exemplars: [
      { level: 1, mark: 3, answer: "Sharing the data is good because the AI could help doctors diagnose diseases like cancer earlier, which could save lives.\n\nBut it could be bad because the data is private and patients didn't agree to it. Because the data is anonymised it is completely safe, as nobody can find out who the patients are. However, companies might make money from it.", comment: "A few relevant points are made on both sides but they are stated rather than explained, which is Level 1. The claim that anonymised data is \"completely safe\" is wrong: anonymised records can often be re-identified by matching them with other data sets, which is one of the key issues the question is pointing at. Explaining re-identification and the legal requirements for health data would reach Level 2." },
      { level: 2, mark: 5, answer: "There are big benefits to sharing the data. AI tools could analyse scans and test results to spot diseases such as cancer earlier and more accurately than doctors, and could reduce the workload of doctors in a very busy NHS. The NHS has one of the largest sets of health data in the world, so the AI tools could be very accurate.\n\nHowever, there are concerns about privacy. Even if names and addresses are removed, it may be possible to work out who someone is by combining the data with other information, such as their postcode, date of birth and the date they went to hospital. Health data is very sensitive, so if it was leaked people could be embarrassed or discriminated against, for example by employers or insurance companies.\n\nPatients might also feel that they did not agree to their data being given to private companies who will make a profit from it.\n\nOverall, the NHS should share the data because the benefits for patients are very large.", comment: "Benefits, re-identification and consent are explained with some development, which is Level 2. The range is limited: there is no legal discussion (health data is special category data under UK GDPR), no mention of bias or accountability for wrong diagnoses, no specific example, and the conclusion ignores the concerns raised. Adding these with examples, and a conclusion that addresses the risks, would reach Level 3." },
      { level: 3, mark: 8, answer: "NHS records are one of the largest health data sets in the world, so AI trained on them could diagnose conditions like cancer or eye disease earlier and more accurately, speed up research and reduce clinicians' workload.\n\nBut \"anonymised\" does not guarantee anonymity. Removing names is not enough if postcode, date of birth and dates of hospital visits remain, because combining these with other data sets can re-identify individuals. Health data is special category data under UK GDPR, so the NHS needs a clear lawful basis, a data protection impact assessment, strong security and data minimisation, sharing only the fields each project needs. Mistakes have happened: in 2017 the ICO found that the Royal Free hospital had broken data protection law when it shared around 1.6 million patient records with Google DeepMind.\n\nTrust is crucial. Patients may accept research but object to companies profiting from their data, so transparency about which companies are involved, why, and what the NHS gets in return matters, along with an easy national opt-out. If the data under-represents some groups, the AI may be less accurate for them, creating bias. And if an AI-assisted diagnosis is wrong, it must be clear whether the company, the clinician or the NHS is accountable.\n\nI think sharing is justified, but only inside a secure data environment where the data never leaves NHS control and companies run their analysis there, with independent oversight and published details of every project. That keeps most of the benefit while greatly reducing the risks of re-identification and loss of trust.", comment: "A logically structured, balanced Level 3 response: re-identification, legal requirements, trust, bias and accountability are developed, with a strong specific example (Royal Free/DeepMind), leading to a justified, conditional conclusion. It is 8 rather than 9 because the bias and accountability points are made briefly without examples, so they are less substantiated than the rest." }
    ] },
    { marks: 12, q: "A company plans to replace many of its customer service staff with an **AI chatbot** and to use software to monitor the productivity of the remaining staff. Discuss the **moral, ethical, legal and cultural** issues.", levels: L12, points: [
      "Benefits: 24/7 service, lower costs, shorter waiting times, staff freed from repetitive queries",
      "Ethical: job losses and inequality; retraining and redeployment; gains to owners rather than workers",
      "Ethical: chatbot errors/hallucinations; customers misled; vulnerable or less digitally confident customers excluded (digital divide); need for human escalation",
      "Ethical/legal: workplace monitoring — proportionality, transparency, lawful basis under UK GDPR; staff informed; data minimisation",
      "Legal: Equality Act 2010 — chatbot must be accessible; reasonable adjustments; productivity metrics may disadvantage disabled staff (indirect discrimination)",
      "Legal: automated decisions about staff (e.g. dismissal based on metrics) — rights around solely automated decisions",
      "Moral: managers' and developers' responsibility for decisions affecting livelihoods; BCS public interest duty",
      "Cultural: erosion of trust and morale; chilling effect; changing expectations of customer service; normalising algorithmic management",
      "Justified conclusion"
    ],
    exemplars: [
      { level: 1, mark: 2, answer: "The chatbot would save the company money because it doesn't need paying. But lots of people will lose their jobs, which is unfair. Monitoring staff is also wrong because it is spying on them and it is illegal.", comment: "A few relevant points are asserted with no explanation or structure, which is Level 1. \"Monitoring is illegal\" is wrong: workplace monitoring can be lawful under UK GDPR if it is necessary, proportionate and transparent to staff. Explaining issues across at least two of the four areas, with the conditions under which monitoring is lawful, would reach Level 2." },
      { level: 2, mark: 5, answer: "There are advantages for the company and its customers. A chatbot can answer questions 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, so customers don't have to wait until the office opens. It can deal with thousands of customers at the same time, so there are no long queues on the phone. It is also much cheaper than paying staff, so the company could reduce its prices.\n\nHowever, there are many ethical problems. The biggest is that many customer service staff will lose their jobs. Customer service jobs are often done by people who do not have many qualifications, so they might find it hard to get another job, especially if other companies are also replacing their staff with AI. This could increase unemployment and inequality, because the company's owners and shareholders get richer while the workers lose their income. The company should offer retraining to staff so they can move to other jobs in the company.\n\nAnother problem is that chatbots make mistakes. AI chatbots sometimes make up information that sounds convincing but is wrong, and customers might believe it. Some customers, such as elderly people or people who are not confident with technology, may find a chatbot frustrating and not be able to get help, so there should always be an option to speak to a human. This is especially true because many people already find automated phone menus frustrating.\n\nChatbots also cannot show empathy in the same way as a real person. If a customer is getting in touch because of a bereavement, a serious problem with their account or a complaint, they may want to speak to someone who understands how they feel, and a chatbot might give a cold or automatic response which makes the situation worse. This could damage the company's reputation, and customers might move to a different company that still has human staff.\n\nMonitoring the remaining staff is also an ethical issue. Staff might feel that they are not trusted and that they are being watched all the time, which would make them stressed and unhappy at work. Productivity software might measure things like how many calls they take or how long they are away from their desk, which does not show how well they actually help customers.\n\nIn conclusion, the company should think carefully before replacing its staff.", comment: "Ethical issues (job losses, chatbot errors, exclusion, monitoring) are explained with some development, which is Level 2, but the answer covers essentially one area. There is nothing legal (UK GDPR on monitoring, the Equality Act, rights around automated decisions) or explicitly moral or cultural, no specific examples, and the conclusion makes no judgement. Covering most of the four areas with developed points and examples would reach Level 3." },
      { level: 3, mark: 8, answer: "A chatbot offers 24/7 service, no queues and lower costs, and frees remaining staff from repetitive questions.\n\n**Ethical.** Many staff will lose their jobs, often people with fewer alternatives, while the savings go to owners and shareholders. Chatbots also make mistakes: in 2024 a Canadian tribunal held Air Canada responsible after its chatbot gave a customer wrong information about bereavement fares, so customers can be misled. Elderly or less digitally confident customers may be excluded unless there is an easy way to reach a human.\n\n**Legal.** Monitoring staff involves processing personal data, so under UK GDPR it must be necessary, proportionate and transparent: staff must be told what is collected and why. In 2024 the ICO ordered Serco Leisure to stop using facial recognition and fingerprints to monitor staff attendance because it was not proportionate. Under the Equality Act 2010, productivity targets could indirectly discriminate against disabled staff, who may need reasonable adjustments, and the chatbot itself must be accessible.\n\n**Moral.** Managers must decide whether saving money justifies the effect on long-serving employees' livelihoods.\n\n**Cultural.** Constant monitoring could damage trust and morale.\n\nOverall the company can use a chatbot but should keep a human option and monitor staff fairly.", comment: "A line of reasoning is followed, with developed ethical and legal points and accurate specific examples (Air Canada, Serco Leisure, Equality Act), while moral and cultural issues are only brief: Level 3. The conclusion is reasonable but does not say what \"fairly\" means or weigh the issues raised. Developing the moral and cultural areas (e.g. algorithmic management, the developers' BCS public-interest duty) and justifying the conclusion would reach Level 4." },
      { level: 4, mark: 11, answer: "The company's case is clear: a chatbot gives 24/7 service without queues, costs far less than staff and can deal with routine questions, leaving people for complex cases. The question is whether this can be done fairly.\n\n**Ethical.** Large job losses fall on staff who may have few alternatives, while savings go to owners, widening inequality. An ethical employer would redeploy and retrain staff where possible. Chatbots can also give confident but wrong answers. In 2024 Air Canada was held liable by a Canadian tribunal after its chatbot misinformed a customer about bereavement fares, so the company is responsible for what its bot says. Vulnerable or less digitally confident customers risk being excluded, so a clear route to a human is essential.\n\n**Legal.** Productivity monitoring processes staff personal data, so under UK GDPR it needs a lawful basis and must be necessary, proportionate and transparent. In 2024 the ICO ordered Serco Leisure to stop using biometrics to monitor staff attendance because less intrusive methods were available. If the company used the metrics to make decisions about staff automatically, such as dismissals, UK GDPR gives individuals rights where decisions with significant effects are based solely on automated processing, including the right to human involvement. The Equality Act 2010 matters twice: the chatbot must be accessible to disabled customers, and uniform productivity targets could indirectly discriminate against disabled staff unless reasonable adjustments are made.\n\n**Moral.** Managers who choose the targets, and the developers who build the monitoring software, are making decisions that affect people's livelihoods. Under the BCS Code, developers should put the public interest first and could raise concerns if the system is designed to be unfair or intrusive.\n\n**Cultural.** Being scored every minute erodes trust and morale and can make staff focus on the metrics rather than the customer. More broadly, it normalises \"algorithmic management\" and changes what customers expect from service: fast and automated, but less personal.\n\nMy conclusion is that the chatbot is justifiable if routine queries go to it, there is always an easy route to a trained human, and displaced staff are retrained or redeployed. Monitoring should be limited to what is proportionate, explained openly to staff, and never used to make decisions about individuals without human review.", comment: "All four areas are covered in depth and applied to the scenario, balanced against the company's case, with accurate legal points and specific examples (Air Canada, Serco Leisure, Equality Act, UK GDPR automated decisions) and a justified, conditional conclusion: Level 4. It is 11 rather than 12 because the cultural section is less substantiated than the others; an example or evidence there would earn the final mark." }
    ] },
    { marks: 3, q: "Describe **three** features of an extended response that would place it in the **top level** of an AQA levels-of-response mark scheme.", scheme: ["Covers all the areas named in the question (e.g. moral, ethical, legal and cultural)", "Points are developed / explained in depth with consequences, not just listed", "Uses specific, relevant examples / legislation applied to the scenario", "Balanced: considers both benefits and risks / counter-arguments", "A coherent, logically structured line of reasoning", "A justified conclusion that weighs the arguments"], maxPoints: 3 },
  ]);
})();
